---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import FigureDefs from '../components/FigureDefs.astro';
import StackFigure from '../components/figures/StackFigure.astro';
import FleetFigure from '../components/figures/FleetFigure.astro';
import InstallerFigure from '../components/figures/InstallerFigure.astro';
import ShellVsTurfFigure from '../components/figures/ShellVsTurfFigure.astro';
import Partner from '../components/Partner.astro';
import Still from '../components/Still.astro';
// Four things people build with Turf, factored by the job (decided 2026-10-20; "use cases", not
// "solutions", since 2026-10-21): each section is
// who it is for, the stack, the HCL (none in 03 since 2026-10-21), day 1 and day 2, the proof, a CTA. Figures are ported from
// Metal to Tokens (components/figures); HCL is quoted from turf-examples where a file exists and
// marked "after …" or "illustrative" where it is adapted. No link to the pre-1.0 demo recordings.
const title = 'Turf — Use cases: GPU clouds, fleets, installers, agents';
const description =
  'What people build with Turf: GPU clouds from metal to tokens, Kubernetes fleets, self-installing products and the agent toolkit, day 1 and day 2 for each.';

const site = 'https://turf.build';
const examples = 'https://github.com/turfbuild/turf-examples/tree/main';
// See demo.astro: inlined, not an `@id` reference to the home page's node.
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Turf', item: `${site}/` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Use cases' },
  ],
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <FigureDefs />
  <section class="subhero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Use cases</p>
      <h1>What people build with Turf.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Turf™ is one engine for the whole stack: hosts, clusters, charts, playbooks and the
        workload in the Terraform® language, planned as one and converged in rounds. Four ways teams put
        it to work, each with its day 1 and its day 2.</p>
      <div class="jumplist">
        <a href="#gpu-clouds"><span class="n">01</span> GPU clouds</a>
        <a href="#kubernetes-fleets"><span class="n">02</span> Kubernetes fleets</a>
        <a href="#installers"><span class="n">03</span> Self-installing products</a>
        <a href="#agents"><span class="n">04</span> The infrastructure agent</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 01 · GPU clouds -->
  <section class="section" id="gpu-clouds">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="band-head">
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> 01 · Neocloud providers</p>
        <h2>GPU clouds, from metal to tokens.</h2>
        <p>A neocloud sells a managed cluster, not GPUs by the hour: qualified before handover, current on
          drivers and firmware, back in service soon after a fault. Turf brings the whole managed cluster
          up in one convergence loop, certification included, and services it on the provider’s runbook.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature center">
        <div>
          <StackFigure />
          <p class="figcap"><b>Every layer is code</b>, brought up by one convergence loop. The decision
            layer, the provider’s agent, runbook and policy, approves each change plan and oversees
            remediation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="codeblock">
          <div class="cb-head"><span>certification.tf</span><span class="cb-legend">certification as a step of the run</span></div>
<pre><span class="kw">resource</span> <span class="str">"kubernetes_manifest"</span> <span class="str">"certification"</span> &#123;
  manifest = &#123;
    apiVersion = <span class="str">"nvcre.nvidia.com/v1alpha1"</span>
    kind       = <span class="str">"Certification"</span>
    spec       = local.spec
  &#125;
  <span class="kw">lifecycle</span> &#123;
    <span class="kw">action_trigger</span> &#123;
      events  = [<span class="ev">before_create</span>]
      actions = [action.kubewait_condition.gpu_census]
    &#125;
    <span class="kw">action_trigger</span> &#123;
      events     = [<span class="ev">after_create</span>]
      actions    = [action.kubewait_condition.certification_complete]
      on_failure = <span class="ev">taint</span>
    &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</pre>
          <div class="cb-note">adapted from turf-examples/use-cases/datacenter/aicr-eks-certification · the census before, the wait after</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="beats two">
        <div class="beat">
          <span class="k">Day 1 · bring-up</span>
          <h3>From pools of inventory to a certified cluster</h3>
          <p>The provider offers hardware as a pool; Turf starts where the pool hands over a machine. The
            host playbook runs as a Terraform action, the cluster comes up, the AI runtime installs as the
            rendered recipe, and the certification runs as a resource with the GPU census before it and
            the wait after. <b>One change plan covers all of it</b>, approved by policy or a person, round
            by round, until nothing is left to defer.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat">
          <span class="k">Day 2 · the runbook</span>
          <h3>One approved step at a time</h3>
          <p>The health checks find the fault. What happens next is the provider’s remediation runbook,
            and the agent follows it: cordon and drain, repair or replace, re-certify, uncordon and
            return, each step a change plan the engine carries out. A recipe upgrade re-fires the
            certification under the same approval, and a fleet is many stacks from a few golden
            recipes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="feature center">
        <div>
          <Still id="certify" src="/console/aicr-round2-certify-action.png" soon
            alt="A preview of the Turf console: round 2 of a deployment run is applying, paused at the certify action, with a lane for every Helm release of the runtime."
            caption="A preview of the Turf console: the AI runtime applying in round 2, paused at the certify step. Click to enlarge." />
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>The reference architecture.</h3>
          <p><em>Metal to Tokens</em> walks the cluster stack three times: one layer at a time, then the
            convergence loop, then day 2 across the data center. Seventeen pages, free, with the stacks
            on GitHub.</p>
          <div class="proof">
            <span class="lab">Proof</span>
            <a href="/reference-architectures/">Metal to Tokens →</a>
            <a href={`${examples}/use-cases/datacenter/aicr-stack`}>aicr-stack →</a>
            <a href={`${examples}/terraform/kubernetes/node-maintenance`}>node-maintenance →</a>
            <a href={`${examples}/use-cases/datacenter/slinky-stack`}>slinky-stack →</a>
            <a href="/engine/">the engine →</a>
          </div>
          <div class="cta-row" style="margin-top:18px">
            <a class="btn primary" href="/reference-architectures/">Read Metal to Tokens</a>
            <a class="btn ghost" href="/get-started/">Get started</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 · Kubernetes fleets -->
  <section class="section alt" id="kubernetes-fleets">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="band-head">
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> 02 · SaaS and platform providers</p>
        <h2>Managed services on Kubernetes fleets.</h2>
        <p>A control plane, a golden recipe, and a fleet of Kubernetes® clusters that each deliver your
          service to a tenant. Turf runs in the cluster beside them: a tenant’s cluster is one module call, a change
          arrives as a pull request or an MCP call, and every cluster is its own stack under approval.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature flip center">
        <div class="codeblock">
          <div class="cb-head"><span>tenants/acme.tf</span><span class="cb-legend">a tenant's cluster: one call to the recipe</span></div>
<pre><span class="kw">module</span> <span class="str">"tenant_acme"</span> &#123;
  source  = <span class="str">"registry.example.com/platform/gpu-cluster/kubernetes"</span>
  version = <span class="str">"2.4.0"</span>

  tenant    = <span class="str">"acme"</span>
  recipe    = <span class="str">"h100-inference"</span>
  gpu_nodes = 16
&#125;

<span class="kw">output</span> <span class="str">"acme"</span> &#123;
  value = module.tenant_acme.endpoints   <span class="cm"># read back by the control plane</span>
&#125;</pre>
          <div class="cb-note">illustrative, after Metal to Tokens, chapter 12 · the golden recipe is a registry module with the variables the provider opens</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <FleetFigure />
          <p class="figcap"><b>A tenant order, in the data center.</b> Each block is a tenant cluster, and the
            green ones have a run in flight.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="beats two">
        <div class="beat">
          <span class="k">Day 1 · a tenant order</span>
          <h3>A module call becomes a cluster</h3>
          <p>The control plane writes the tenant’s cluster as HCL and opens a pull request, or calls Turf’s
            MCP server directly: <code>open</code>, <code>up</code>, <code>apply</code>, <code>output</code>.
            Either way the change is a plan, approved before it runs, and the outputs come back to the
            control plane when the run converges. <b>Turf runs in Kubernetes</b>, deployed as an agent,
            with its state in the cluster.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat">
          <span class="k">Day 2 · the fleet</span>
          <h3>Upgrades, cluster by cluster, under one approval policy</h3>
          <p>Bump the recipe version and every cluster plans its own change; routine changes are approved
            by policy, the rest by a person. Drift is found on every plan. Replacing a cluster plans the
            orderly teardown of what it hosted instead of letting it vanish with the machine, and a
            run that is interrupted picks up where it stopped.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="proof">
        <span class="lab">Proof</span>
        <a href={`${examples}/integrations/kagent`}>Turf in the cluster, via kagent →</a>
        <a href={`${examples}/integrations/kubernetes-backend`}>state in a Kubernetes secret →</a>
        <a href="/reference-architectures/">Metal to Tokens, chapter 12 →</a>
        <a href="/engine/">the engine →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="cta-row" style="margin-top:18px">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/get-started/">Get started</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="/licensing/#pilot">Start a pilot</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 · Products that install themselves -->
  <section class="section" id="installers">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="band-head">
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> 03 · ISVs and AI-native products</p>
        <h2>Products that install themselves.</h2>
        <p>Your product ships a reference architecture; the customer gets a forty-page deployment guide
          and a solutions engineer. Make the installer part of the product instead: the architecture as
          Terraform modules, planned in the customer’s account before anything exists, with the
          installer’s own steps written in as turf actions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="figwide">
        <InstallerFigure />
        <p class="figcap"><b>The installer is code too.</b> The product ships its architecture and its own
          steps; Turf plans in the customer's account with no credentials, the customer approves, and the
          loop creates identity, compute, network and storage until the app is up.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="beats two">
        <div class="beat">
          <span class="k">Day 1 · the install</span>
          <h3>Planned before anything exists</h3>
          <p>The reference architecture is Terraform modules the product ships. In the customer’s account
            Turf plans it from empty, the customer approves, and the apply runs with the installer’s
            steps inside it: verify the storage is private, send the welcome when the app is usable.
            <b>Any mix of HCL and natural language</b>: the modules carry the architecture, the customer’s
            specifics arrive as variables or as a sentence.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat">
          <span class="k">Day 2 · the same plan, again</span>
          <h3>Upgrades and drift, under the same approval</h3>
          <p>A new version of the product is a new plan in the customer’s account. Drift is detected on
            every plan and healed by the same loop. Nothing that holds the customer’s data is removed
            without their word, because <code>consent</code> is bound before its destroy. The product
            drives Turf over MCP, the same interface Claude Code uses, and runs where the product runs:
            embedded, in a container, in the cluster, on a local model.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="proof">
        <span class="lab">Proof</span>
        <a href={`${examples}/use-cases/products/data-lake-installer`}>data-lake-installer →</a>
        <a href="/get-started/#connect">drive Turf over MCP →</a>
        <a href="/compatibility/">the compatibility matrix →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="cta-row" style="margin-top:18px">
        <a class="btn primary" href="mailto:contact@turf.build?subject=Autonomous%20products">Talk to us</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="/get-started/">Get started</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 · The ultimate infrastructure agent -->
  <section class="section alt" id="agents">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="band-head">
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> 04 · Teams adopting agents</p>
        <h2>The ultimate infrastructure agent.</h2>
        <p>Turf is an MCP server at its core: the tools and the skills an agent needs to take a sentence
          to a running stack and to operate it afterwards, with the plan as the contract between them.
          Designed for efficient reasoning models such as Claude Sonnet 5.5: the skills carry the
          procedure, the results carry the summary, and the model carries the judgment.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature center">
        <div>
          <ShellVsTurfFigure />
          <p class="figcap"><b>The same agent, with a shell or with Turf.</b> The engine carries it out; the
            agent oversees, and every step leaves a record.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="term" role="img" aria-label="A session transcript: the agent searches for the AWS provider, describes a resource type, writes the configuration, initializes, opens a workspace, plans, checks the plan against policy and applies it.">
            <div class="term-head"><span>a session, from a sentence</span></div>
            <div class="term-body">
<div class="t"><span class="p">&gt;</span> <span class="b">deploy a data lake for tenant acme with Turf</span></div>
<div class="t"><span class="dim">provider search</span>   "aws"          <span class="dim">→</span> hashicorp/aws</div>
<div class="t"><span class="dim">provider describe</span> aws_s3_bucket  <span class="dim">→</span> schema · identity</div>
<div class="t"><span class="dim">(the authoring skill writes versions.tf, lake.tf)</span></div>
<div class="t"><span class="dim">init</span>              ./lake         <span class="dim">→</span> providers locked</div>
<div class="t"><span class="dim">open</span>              workspace acme</div>
<div class="t"><span class="dim">up</span>                               <span class="dim">→</span> <span class="amber">approval point</span> · 7 to add</div>
<div class="t"><span class="dim">show</span>              → input.tfplan <span class="dim">→</span> policy: no denials</div>
<div class="t"><span class="dim">apply</span>             by its digest  <span class="dim">→</span> <span class="ok">converged</span> · outputs</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="codeblock" style="margin-top:14px">
            <div class="cb-head"><span>lake.tf</span><span class="cb-legend">from the console: the agent writes the import block</span></div>
<pre><span class="kw">import</span> &#123;
  to = aws_s3_bucket.raw
  id = <span class="str">"acme-raw-data"</span>
&#125;

<span class="kw">resource</span> <span class="str">"aws_s3_bucket"</span> <span class="str">"raw"</span> &#123;
  bucket = <span class="str">"acme-raw-data"</span>
&#125;</pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="toolgrid">
        <div class="tool"><b>plan: up · destroy · import · refresh</b>Every change is a plan: converge the
          directory, tear it down, adopt what exists by id or identity, or read the world back into state.
          Each stops at its approval point with the plan's summary.</div>
        <div class="tool"><b>approve: apply, by digest</b><code>apply</code> with the plan's digest is the
          approval, of that plan and no other. Not calling it declines; <code>cancel</code> ends the run.
          Auto-approval only where you said so.</div>
        <div class="tool"><b>the planfile, for integration</b><code>show</code> returns the plan as
          OpenTofu's own document: <code>input.tfplan</code> for OPA, conftest or Checkov, or your
          platform's policy groups, before a non-trivial plan is approved.</div>
        <div class="tool"><b>apply, with interaction points</b>The engine carries the plan out and stops at
          every turf action it meets: a backup, a check, a question, a notification, answered by the agent
          and recorded with the run.</div>
        <div class="tool"><b>invoke: action-only plans, for day 2</b>One declared action, or an ad-hoc one
          for this run alone, planned by itself under its own approval point: reboot, backup, cordon,
          re-certify.</div>
        <div class="tool"><b>state: list · show · output · taint</b>State, outputs and plans as OpenTofu's
          own documents, sensitive values redacted unless asked; <code>taint</code> marks a replacement for
          the next plan; workspaces listed and deleted.</div>
        <div class="tool"><b>provider search · describe</b>Any provider on the registry, and the exact
          schema of a type: required, computed, write-only, identity. Provider-linked skills
          <span class="pill soon">coming soon</span></div>
        <div class="tool"><b>resource query</b>Bulk discovery with <code>list</code> and <code>query</code>,
          for import at scale.<span class="pill soon">coming soon</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="beats two">
        <div class="beat">
          <span class="k">Day 1 · from a sentence</span>
          <h3>To a running stack, in your directory</h3>
          <p>The agent finds the provider, reads the schema it will write against, and writes ordinary
            Terraform files you can read, commit and run anywhere. <code>up</code> plans the whole
            directory and stops at the approval point with the plan’s summary; <b>you approve from Claude
            Code, Cursor, Codex or any MCP client</b>, on the subscription you already have, and the
            engine carries it out.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat">
          <span class="k">Day 2 · the same agent operates</span>
          <h3>Import, refresh, invoke, answer, check</h3>
          <p>What was clicked together comes in through <code>import</code>. Drift shows on every plan. A declared action
            runs on demand with its own approval point. The guardrails example scans every provider
            before it runs, pins the workload to a commit and checks every plan against policy from the
            planfile. Local models and private GPU clouds: <b>intent never has to leave your network</b>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="proof">
        <span class="lab">Proof</span>
        <a href="/get-started/#connect">connect your agent →</a>
        <a href={`${examples}/use-cases/guardrails`}>guardrails →</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/turfbuild/turf-examples">the guided journeys, /turf:guide →</a>
        <a href="/compatibility/">the compatibility matrix →</a>
        <a href="/engine/">the engine →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="cta-row" style="margin-top:18px">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/get-started/#connect">Connect your agent</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="/get-started/">Get started</a>
      </div>
      <p class="legal-note">NVIDIA, AI Cluster Runtime, NVCRE, NVSentinel and KAI Scheduler are trademarks or product
        names of NVIDIA Corporation, referred to here only to describe interoperability. Amazon EKS, Amazon S3,
        Google Kubernetes Engine, Google Cloud Storage, Azure Kubernetes Service and Azure Blob Storage are
        trademarks of their respective owners. GitHub, GitLab, JFrog Artifactory, HashiCorp Vault, Scalr and
        Spacelift are trademarks of their respective owners, named here only to describe interoperability.
        Claude is a trademark of Anthropic, PBC. Turf is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored by
        any of them. All other marks belong to their owners.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Partner />
</Layout>
